//
// A VECTOR OF `&` RESULTS TAKES THE JOIN OF THEIR RANGES (`@FR-G-Amp-Infix`, crossed with
// `@FR-I-Join` and `@FR-T-Chk-Vec`).  `a & k` synthesises a RANGE type (`a & 1` is `integer
// limit(0, 1)`, `a & 2` is `limit(0, 2)`).  An unannotated vector literal of such elements
// has no expected element type, so its element type must hold EVERY element: the join, as
// `(I-Join)` gives a variable written several times ("never just the first/narrowest").
//
// ⚠ DEFECT (reported by the rule walk, 2026-10-18): the element type is the FIRST element's
// range, and every wider element is refused: `w = [a & 1, a & 2]` is "cannot implicitly
// narrow integer limit(0, 2) to integer limit(0, 1)" on both backends; `[a % 2, a % 3]` is
// the same with `limit(-1, 1)`.  Widest first (`[a & 4, a & 1]`) compiles, and an annotated
// `w: vector<integer> = [a & 1, a & 2]` does too.  Not specific to `&`: any range-typed
// first element.  No rule states the synthesised element type of a vector literal; `(I-Join)`
// is the nearest.
//
// @IGNORE — the defect above: a vector literal's element type is its first element's range.
// @falsified-at: none — the expected behaviour does not exist on any build yet; this cell is
//   the receipt the fix must turn green.
// CHANNEL: the asserts (today: a compile refusal).
// ARMED: no instrument.
// WITNESS: today "cannot implicitly narrow integer limit(0, 2) to integer limit(0, 1)".
// HOLDS: the widest-first and annotated spellings keep compiling to the same values.

fn test_narrowest_first() {
  a = 6;
  w = [a & 1, a & 2, a & 4];
  assert(w == [0, 2, 4] && len(w) == 3, "{w}");
  m = [a % 2, a % 4];
  assert(m == [0, 2] && len(m) == 2, "{m}");
}
